    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Tartessos was the first entity in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> recognised as a kingdom, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> names its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arganthonios">Arganthonios</a> as the host of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> to reach Iberia.</li><li>Its wealth lay in metals: by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> extraction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huelva">Huelva</a> Province had reached industrial proportions.</li><li>Scholars describe it as a hybrid archaeological culture, formed around the 9th century BC through contact between Phoenician settlers and local people.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Excavations on two adjoining lots in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huelva">Huelva</a>, together 2,150 square metres, yielded some 90,000 fragments of local, Phoenician and Greek pottery dated from the 10th to the early 8th centuries BC.</p></aside>
    </section>

